 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by region growing and statistical computation. For $N$ voxels and $K$ regions, computing means and variances is $O(N)$. Occlusion checks are $O(K^2)$. Pre-processing times reported were ~150-215 seconds for ~256^3 volumes on CPU [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing region membership for all voxels and pre-computed statistics per region. $O(N)$ for voxel data, $O(K)$ for region stats.
-*   **Strengths:** Automatically reveals internal structures obscured by larger outer shells without manual tuning. Enhances boundary details via gradient modulation.
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate segmentation/clustering. Performance degrades if regions are numerous ($K$ is large) due to $O(K^2)$ occlusion checks. Color assignment is heuristic and may not reflect physical material properties.
-*   **When to Use:** Volumetric datasets with distinct, clustered structures where internal visibility is critical (e.g., medical CT/MRI with overlapping tissues).
-*   **When Not to Use:** Data without clear spatial clustering or when physical material color is known and required.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Computing region statistics is $O(N)$ where $N$ is total voxels. Determining occlusions is $O(K^2)$ where $K$ is the number of regions. Rendering is $O(N)$.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store voxel data and region membership. $O(K)$ for region statistics.
+*   **Performance:** In experiments on 16-bit CT volumes (e.g., 256x256x161), pre-processing took ~150-215 seconds on a 2.66 GHz CPU. Real-time frame rates were achieved on GPU-based ray marching at 512x512 resolution [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Automatically reveals small, interior structures occluded by large ones without manual intervention. Enhances boundary visibility via gradient modulation.
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate segmentation/clustering. Color assignment is heuristic (size-based) and may not reflect semantic material properties. The occlusion model is simplified (based on mean distance and size, not ray-casting depth).
+*   **When to use:** When the goal is to visualize internal structures of complex, multi-scale volumes where manual TF tuning is difficult.
+*   **When not to use:** When semantic accuracy of color is critical (requires external knowledge) or when regions are not well-separated by size/position.
 
 ## Background
-This method was proposed in the context of automatic transfer function design using clustering on LH (Local Histogram) space [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the difficulty of manually tuning transfer functions for complex volumetric data where structures overlap. The approach leverages the geometric properties of segmented clusters to derive visual parameters, ensuring that smaller, potentially occluded structures remain visible.
+This method was proposed in the context of automatic transfer function design using clustering on LH (Local Histogram) space [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the difficulty of assigning visual parameters to clusters derived from volume data, particularly when significant overlap exists between clusters. The approach leverages the intuition that smaller structures are more likely to be occluded and thus require higher opacity, while larger structures should be more transparent to reveal interiors.
 
